            <ArticleTitle>Effects of a resistance training program on balance and fatigue perception in patients with Parkinson's disease: A randomized controlled trial.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0025-7753(17)30823-0</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.medcli.2017.10.022</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="INTRODUCTION AND OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Fatigue and balance impairment leads to a loss of independence and are important to adequately manage. The objective of this study was to examine the effects of a resistance training program on dynamic balance and fatigue in patients with Parkinson's disease (PD).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Randomized controlled trial. Forty-six patients with PD were randomly allocated to an intervention group receiving a 8-week resistance training program focused on lower limbs or to a control group. Balance was assessed using the Mini-BESTest and fatigue was assessed by the Piper Fatigue Scale.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Patients in the intervention group improved significantly (p&lt;0.05) on dynamic balance (reactive postural control and total values) and perceived fatigue.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">An 8-week resistance training program was found to be effective at improving dynamic balance and fatigue in patients with PD.</AbstractText>
